# Drive Qualification Automation

Drive Qualification Automation is the Apricorn storage qualification workflow
for creating report JSON, collecting qualification artifacts, and generating the
final Word report.

The primary entrypoint is:

```bash
uv run drive-qual
```

Running `drive-qual` with no arguments, or with `--help`, prints the project man
page with the supported commands, workflow order, and examples.

## Quick Start

Install the project dependencies:

```bash
uv venv
uv sync --group dev
```

Show the command man page:

```bash
uv run drive-qual
```

Start a qualification report:

```bash
uv run drive-qual start --part-number 69-420
```

Run individual workflow steps:

```bash
uv run drive-qual step equipment --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual step power --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual step performance --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual step temperature --part-number 69-420
```

Generate the Word report:

```bash
uv run drive-qual report --part-number 69-420
```

## Command Reference

The maintained CLI is `drive-qual`.

```text
drive-qual
drive-qual <command> [options]
```

Common commands:

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `drive-qual start` | Create a new report session by collecting drive info. |
| `drive-qual run` | Run the maintained workflow sequence. |
| `drive-qual resume` | Resume an orchestrated workflow from its manifest. |
| `drive-qual step <name>` | Run one workflow step. |
| `drive-qual report` | Generate the Word report from report JSON and artifacts. |
| `drive-qual temperature` | Post-process temperature CSV/chart artifacts. |
| `drive-qual status` | Show the active session and report path. |
| `drive-qual list-steps` | Show workflow step names and friendly aliases. |
| `drive-qual list-profiles` | Show named workflow profiles. |
| `drive-qual legacy` | Run the old Windows-focused legacy entrypoint. |

Step aliases:

| Alias | Workflow step |
| --- | --- |
| `info` | `drive_info` |
| `equipment` | `equipment` |
| `power` | `power_measurements` |
| `performance` / `perf` | `performance` |
| `temperature` / `temp` | `temperature` |

Examples:

```bash
uv run drive-qual run --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual resume --profile core_perf_temp_v1 --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual step power --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual temperature --part-number 69-420 --dut "Padlock DT" --csv matched.csv --chart temp.png
uv run drive-qual report --part-number 69-420 --output C:\Reports\69-420.docx
```

The older compatibility commands still exist:

```bash
uv run drive-qual-report
uv run drive-qual-report-generate
uv run drive-qual-temperature
```

New technician instructions should use `drive-qual`.

## Qualification Workflow

The report is enriched step by step. Later steps assume the earlier report and
session scaffolding already exist.

1. `drive_info`
   Creates the report folder, collects core drive metadata, writes the initial
   report JSON, and sets the current session marker.
2. `equipment`
   Records host, scope, probe, and DUT metadata. It also ensures the report has
   `power`, `performance`, and `temperature` sections.
3. `power_measurements`
   Runs the In Rush and Max IO measurement flow, captures scope artifacts, and
   writes compatibility and power results back into the report.
4. `performance`
   Collects platform performance data:
   Windows uses CrystalDiskInfo, CrystalDiskMark, and ATTO;
   Linux uses the native Disks benchmark wrapper;
   macOS uses Blackmagic Disk Speed Test with automation and manual fallback.
5. `temperature`
   Post-processes matched temperature/performance rows and a temperature chart
   into the report JSON and artifact folder.
6. `report`
   Generates `drive_qualification_report.docx` from the completed report folder.

The normal technician sequence is:

```bash
uv run drive-qual start --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual step equipment --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual step power --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual step performance --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual step temperature --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual report --part-number 69-420
```

For an orchestrated run with resume support:

```bash
uv run drive-qual run --profile core_perf_temp_v1 --part-number 69-420
uv run drive-qual resume --profile core_perf_temp_v1 --part-number 69-420
```

## Reports and Artifacts

The workflow stores report data and collected artifacts under the configured
artifact root. In the lab this is typically the `Z:\` share.

Common paths:

| Artifact | Path |
| --- | --- |
| Report JSON | `Z:\<part_number>\drive_qualification_report_atomic_tests.json` |
| Word report | `Z:\<part_number>\drive_qualification_report.docx` |
| In Rush CSV/PNG | `Z:\<part_number>\<OS>\In Rush Current\...` |
| Max IO CSV/PNG | `Z:\<part_number>\<OS>\Max IO\...` |
| Windows performance | `Z:\<part_number>\Windows\<tool>\...` |
| Linux performance | `Z:\<part_number>\Linux\Disks\...` |
| macOS performance | `Z:\<part_number>\macOS\Blackmagic Disk Speed Test\...` |
| Temperature chart | `Z:\<part_number>\Temperature\<DUT> Temperature Data.png` |

For offline report generation from a copied folder, pass the parent folder with
`--source-root`:

```bash
uv run drive-qual report --part-number 69-420 --source-root C:\Reports
```

The copied folder should preserve this layout:

```text
C:\Reports\69-420\drive_qualification_report_atomic_tests.json
```

## Host and Lab Requirements

Base requirements:

- Python 3.12
- `uv`
- access to the configured report/artifact root

Technician workflow requirements vary by step and host:

- signed Apricorn `usb` CLI available on `PATH`
- Tektronix scope reachable over SCPI/TCP for power capture
- scope-visible artifact share mounted at `Z:\`
- `fio` available on `PATH` or bundled under `tools`
- `diskspd.exe` available for Windows paths that use it
- CrystalDiskInfo installed at its configured Windows path
- CrystalDiskMark installed at its configured Windows path
- ATTO Disk Benchmark installed at its configured Windows path
- Blackmagic Disk Speed Test installed on macOS when using that path

The current Tektronix integration defaults to `10.10.10.3`. Verify the actual
lab network before changing instrument connectivity settings.

## Platform Support

Shared report/session helpers, path normalization, report generation,
post-processing, and tests are intended to run on Windows, Linux, and macOS.

Some technician steps are platform or lab specific:

- Windows: PowerShell disk operations, `pywinauto`, CrystalDiskInfo,
  CrystalDiskMark, ATTO, and `diskspd`.
- Linux: native Disks benchmark wrapper and Linux block-device handling.
- macOS: Blackmagic Disk Speed Test automation and SMB mount handling.

Shared CLI commands such as `drive-qual`, `drive-qual list-steps`, and
`drive-qual report` should not import Windows-only modules unless the selected
runtime path actually needs them.

## Troubleshooting

Check the active session:

```bash
uv run drive-qual status
```

List supported steps and aliases:

```bash
uv run drive-qual list-steps
```

List orchestrated workflow profiles:

```bash
uv run drive-qual list-profiles
```

Generate a report from copied data:

```bash
uv run drive-qual report --part-number 69-420 --source-root C:\Reports
```

If a shared command fails on Linux or macOS because it imports a Windows-only
dependency, treat that as an import-boundary bug.

## Development

Install development dependencies:

```bash
uv sync --group dev
uv run pre_commit install
```

Run tests:

```bash
uv run pytest
```

Run the full pre-commit suite:

```bash
uv run pre_commit run --all-files
```

Key modules:

| Module | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `drive_qual.cli.main` | Primary `drive-qual` command dispatcher and man page. |
| `drive_qual.workflows.report` | Maintained report workflow orchestration. |
| `drive_qual.workflows.drive_info` | Initial report creation and drive metadata prompts. |
| `drive_qual.workflows.equipment` | Equipment metadata and DUT section scaffolding. |
| `drive_qual.platforms.power_measurements` | Platform-neutral power step entrypoint. |
| `drive_qual.platforms.performance` | Platform-neutral performance dispatcher. |
| `drive_qual.workflows.temperature` | Temperature post-processing workflow. |
| `drive_qual.reports.generate` | Word report generation. |
| `drive_qual.core.report_session` | Report paths and current-session marker. |
| `drive_qual.core.storage_paths` | Artifact path construction and localization. |

The tests cover path/report logic, import boundaries, report generation, and
mocked workflow behavior. They do not replace lab validation for hardware,
scope, GUI automation, or benchmark-tool behavior.
